Pe dată de 2011-07-13 23:26, Janis Papanagnou a scris:
> On 13.07.2011 20:55, Michael Deutschmann wrote:
>> Really, the problem isn't actually specific to floating eyes.  No one
>> ever forgets that meleeing blue "e"s is a bad idea -- the problem is
>> that in the early game where you have no light, *any* monster can appear
>> in your way with a single turn's warning.  Floating eyes are just the
>> first problematic monster for a non-pacifist, non-protection-racket
>> player.
>>
>> The "least cheaty" (for those who think slowing down and relying on the
>> travel command is the only true course) of the existing patches is
>> "justmove".  It turns every move into an "m"-prefixed move.  But that
>> means if you accidentally bump a monster, you waste a turn in front of
>> it.  Which can be bad for things other than eyes.
> Hmm..
> 
> I may be misunderstanding you patch, but it seems not really necessary
> because there are so many ways to move, not only the ordinary [prefixed]
> single moves but - besides the "complex" travel command - also the
> g-prefixed moves. Actually, the <g>-<dir> sequence is the variant of
> move that I seem to constantly use in all the predominant cases where
> I move more than a single square. The travel command, BTW, is useful on
> explored levels (only?), and it can be made significantly less "complex"
> to apply by choosing a "landmark" as target, say an altar (sequence "__."),
> or a stair (sequence "_>." or "_<." resp.), or a throne, or a fountain,
> etc. Then there's also the <M>-<dir> (with a capital M) to move more than
> one square. (And you can prefix the move commands also with a repetition
> count, if you like, for specific situations.)
> 
> For keep-your-fingers-on-a-key-to-move-many-squares players your patch
> may certainly be quite useful. But wouldn't applying one of the existing
> variants of moving commands already solve the issue that you addressed
> with your patch? (Curious what the RGRN folks think.)
> 
> Janis
Actually, I had the impression that punishing the, as you put it,
keep-your-fingers-on-a-key-to-move-many-squares players was an
intentional part of the game.  This would mean that the patch removes
that part (which might help such players early, but will probably kill
them more often in the long term, since thinking before making a move
definitely is a quality to be learned sooner or later…


